  "focus": "Class 10 is the first full board-examination year: the entire syllabus is tested in timed papers, so preparation must shift from chapter completion to answer writing, revision cycles and speed. High-weightage chapters like Chemical Reactions, Life Processes, Light, Electricity, Real Numbers and Trigonometry deserve first-pass mastery, followed by sample papers and previous year questions under exam conditions.",
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How should I plan Class 10 preparation across the year?",
      "answer": "Finish the syllabus once with notes and NCERT exercises, then run two revision cycles — important questions chapter-wise first, then timed sample papers and previous year papers subject-wise."
    },
    {
      "question": "Which Class 10 subjects need the most practice?",
      "answer": "Science and Mathematics reward written practice the most: diagrams, derivations, numericals and proofs. Languages reward consistent writing practice and grammar drills."
    }
  ]
